cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Compartment-Resolved Spatial Transcriptomics of the NSCLC Tumour Immune
  Microenvironment: Primary Lung Tumour vs. Brain Metastasis
message: >-
  If you use this workflow or its derived outputs, please cite it as below, and
  cite the primary data separately (GEO GSE200563; PMID 36216799). The software
  this workflow depends on is listed under `references`, each at the version
  recorded in workflow/envs/*.osx-arm64.pin.txt — p6t3_citation_audit asserts
  that agreement on every build, because a hand-typed version goes stale
  silently.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Jarrett
    family-names: Evans
    email: jevans2532@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  A reproducible Snakemake workflow reanalysing NanoString GeoMx DSP whole
  transcriptome atlas profiles (GEO GSE200563; 120 AOIs, 35 NSCLC patients plus
  7 non-tumour brain controls) to compare the tumour immune microenvironment of
  primary lung tumours with that of matched brain metastases, resolved by
  tissue compartment. The brain immune compartment is n = 8 AOIs, which detects
  roughly 1.1-1.3 SD at 80% power, so a null result is uninformative rather
  than negative; detection is the dominant limitation ahead of even that. The
  assay carries no coordinates, so cross-compartment findings are inferred
  crosstalk between adjacent compartments, never colocalisation. A learning and
  methods project, not intended for peer-reviewed publication.
keywords:
  - spatial transcriptomics
  - GeoMx DSP
  - non-small cell lung cancer
  - brain metastasis
  - tumour immune microenvironment
  - Snakemake
  - FAIR
  - reproducible research
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/jjevans25/NSCLC_BM_Spatial_Transcriptomics"
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-16"
# NO DOI, BY DECISION (ADR 0032). P6-T5 -- the Zenodo release -- is abandoned
# rather than deferred, so cite this workflow by repository URL and version,
# which is what a citation of it can honestly be.
#
# The field is ABSENT rather than a placeholder string, permanently: a DOI that
# does not resolve is worse than no DOI. p6t3_citation_audit fails the build if
# this file claims one while config.yaml has none, and fails it the other way
# round too, so minting one later is a config edit plus a `doi:` line here.
references:
  # EVERY `version:` BELOW IS QUOTED, and that is not style. YAML reads `2.0_6`
  # as the FLOAT 2.06 — an underscore is a digit separator — so lme4 and
  # lmerTest were silently rewritten to 2.06 and 3.21 on the first pass, and
  # p6t3_citation_audit caught both against the pins. R versions use that
  # underscore form throughout.
  # --- the data and the study that produced it -----------------------------
  - type: database
    title: >-
      Spatial transcriptomic profiling of matched primary NSCLC and brain
      metastasis (NanoString GeoMx DSP WTA)
    authors:
      - name: "See PMID 36216799"
    url: "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE200563"
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "GEO:GSE200563"
    notes: >-
      The primary data. Retrieved and verified against pinned SHA-256 digests by
      P0-T2; P3-T2b checks this project's inputs against the article's own
      deposited Source Data and finds all 2,243,280 normalised values identical.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Source publication for GEO GSE200563
    authors:
      - name: "See PMID 36216799"
    url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36216799/"
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "PMID:36216799"
    notes: >-
      This workflow is an independent reanalysis, not a reproduction of the
      published pipeline. Gate 2 reproduced the published direction.

